# 36. Installing packages

`hql package` builds a package from its crate and installs it into a
project, or into the HQL home, where every program on the machine reaches
it. This chapter is the commands, the files they write, and the settings of
`.hql/config.toml` that concern packages.

## The commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `hql package install <name>` | build a package the registry lists, install it into the project, declare it and pin it ([[37-the-registry-and-publishing#installing-by-name|chapter 37]]) |
| `hql package install --path <dir>` | build the package of a crate on this machine and install it into the project |
| `hql package install` | install every package the project declares, at the version it pins |
| `hql package list` | the packages installed in the project and in the HQL home, each with its modules and its level |
| `hql package info <name>` | what an installed package records about itself |
| `hql package rebuild [<name>]` | build an installed package again from its source; every installed package where none is named |
| `hql package remove <name>` | remove an installed package, its declaration and its pin |

`install`, `rebuild`, `update` and `remove` act on the project: the one
`--project` names, else the nearest directory at or above the working
directory holding `.hql/config.toml`. With `--home` they act on the HQL home
instead, as `npm install -g` acts on the machine rather than the project.
Without a project, each refuses and names `--home`.

This chapter installs from a directory, which needs no network and no
registry. Installing needs `cargo` on the `PATH`
([rustup](https://rustup.rs) installs it). Listing, describing and
removing need nothing but `hql`.

## Installing

Inside a project, here one whose `.hql/config.toml` exists and is empty:

```sh
cd ~/code/reading-club
hql package install --path ~/hql/tests/packages/hello
```

```text
   Compiling hql-ext-hello v0.1.0
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 58.20s
installed hello 0.1.0 (hello) in /home/ada/code/reading-club/.hql/packages/hello
```

The first build of a package compiles the `hql` crate and its
dependencies, which takes about a minute; later builds reuse them. The
last line is written once the package has been built, installed and
loaded: a package that builds and cannot be loaded is reported here, with
the reason, and not left for a program to find.

```sh
hql package list
```

```text
hello 0.1.0 (hello) [project]
```

```sh
hql eval 'import hello
hello.greet("Ada")'
```

```text
"Hello, Ada!"
```

## What is installed

A project's `.hql/` and the HQL home, `~/.hql` unless `$HQL_HOME` names
another, have the same layout, and a package installs into either:

```text
.hql/
├── config.toml          declares the packages, by name
├── lock.toml            pins each to a version and a source
├── packages/
│   └── hello/
│       ├── package.toml
│       ├── modules/
│       │   └── hello/mod.hql
│       └── libhql_ext_hello.dylib
└── cache/               sources fetched to build from
```

`install` declares the package by name in `config.toml`, with no version:

```toml
[packages]
dependencies = ["hello"]
```

and pins it in `lock.toml`, which `hql package` writes:

```toml
[[package]]
name = "hello"
version = "0.1.0"
source = "path"
path = "/home/ada/hql/tests/packages/hello"
```

A project commits `config.toml` and `lock.toml`, and leaves `packages/` and
`cache/` out of version control. `hql package install` with no name builds
them again: every declared package at the version its pin names, and a
declared package with no pin at the newest version this `hql` admits. A run
whose project declares a package that is not installed fails before any
program is checked, and names `hql package install`.

`package.toml` is the record of the build:

```toml
[package]
name = "hello"
crate = "hql-ext-hello"
version = "0.1.0"
provides = ["hello"]

[source]
kind = "path"
path = "/home/ada/hql/tests/packages/hello"

[build]
hql = "0.7.0"
rustc = "1.98.1 (48a229cea 2026-09-01)"
target = "aarch64-apple-darwin"
library = "libhql_ext_hello.dylib"
built = "2026-11-14T10:02:11Z"
```

| Table | Records |
| --- | --- |
| `[package]` | the package, its crate and version, and the modules it provides |
| `[source]` | where the source came from, which is what `rebuild` builds again |
| `[build]` | the `hql` and the compiler it was built with, and the library's file name |

The package's directory is self-contained. Removing it, with its declaration
and its pin, is what `hql package remove` does. The crate it was built from
is not needed again until a rebuild.

## Installing for every project

`--home` installs into the HQL home instead:

```sh
hql package install --home --path ~/hql/tests/packages/hello
```

```text
installed hello 0.1.0 (hello) in /home/ada/.hql/packages/hello
```

The home has its own `config.toml` and `lock.toml`, and its packages reach
every run on the machine, with a project or without one. A run looks for a
package in the project first and in the home second, so a project's pinned
version is the one its programs use.

## Describing and rebuilding

```sh
hql package info hello
```

```text
package: hello
crate: hql-ext-hello 0.1.0
provides: hello
source: path /home/ada/hql/tests/packages/hello
built: 2026-11-14T10:02:11Z against hql 0.7.0 with rustc 1.98.1 (48a229cea 2026-09-01) for aarch64-apple-darwin
library: /home/ada/code/reading-club/.hql/packages/hello/libhql_ext_hello.dylib
```

After `hql` is upgraded, an installed package was built for the previous
one and is refused until it is rebuilt
([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/the-version-rule|the version rule]]).
`hql package list` says which:

```text
hello 0.1.0 (hello) [project]: built against hql 0.7.0, run `hql package rebuild hello`
```

```sh
hql package rebuild
```

Without a name, `rebuild` builds every installed package again, which is
what an upgrade of `hql` asks for.

`hql doctor` reports the packages of the environment a program in the
current directory would run in, each as loaded or refused:

```text
package  ok      hello 0.1.0 (hello)
```

## A package under development

Installing copies a build. While a package is being written, a project
names the crate instead, under `[packages] paths` in its `.hql/config.toml`:

```toml
[packages]
paths = ["../hello"]
```

A path is read against the project root, the directory holding `.hql/`. Each
crate listed is built the first time a program runs in the project, and
again whenever its `Cargo.toml`, a file under `src/` or a file under its
module root is newer than the build. The build is kept under the crate's own
`target/` directory, and nothing is installed or pinned: a crate on disk is
never pinned.

<!-- run: --project examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

Greeting("Ada").length
```

```text
11
```

The project of that program is
[`examples/packages/`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/tree/main/examples/packages),
whose `.hql/config.toml` lists the `hello` crate. A crate a project lists comes
before an installed package of the same name, so the checkout being edited
is the one that runs. [A local crate](a-local-crate.hmd) follows one crate
from its first build to an installed package.

## Importing a package for every program

A module of a package is imported where it is used, as any module outside
the prelude is. A project whose programs all use one may import it once,
under `[modules] import` in `.hql/config.toml`, by the package's name:

```toml
[modules]
import = ["hello"]
```

Every program of the project then starts with the package's modules
imported, and `hello.greet("Ada")` needs no `import` line. The setting names a
standard module or a package; it is the one place a package is named. An
entry may also be a `from` statement, `"from hello import greet"`, and then
`greet("Ada")` is written without the module's name.

## Where a module comes from

| Source | Found in | Wins over |
| --- | --- | --- |
| a builtin package | the `hql` binary | everything: no package provides a module `std` or `hmd` does |
| a crate under `[packages] paths` | the project's `.hql/config.toml` | an installed package of the same name |
| a package installed in the project | `.hql/packages/` of the project | the same package installed in the HQL home |
| a package installed in the HQL home | `~/.hql/packages/` | a project module of the same name |
| a project module | the module roots ([[05-modules|chapter 5]]) | |

`hql builtins` prints the module graph a program in the current directory
would see, each module with the package and version providing it.

